WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
A few grams of bile acid help absorb the fat in every meal.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.020
How can so little go so far?

3
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.620
For water, what left the gut was whatever was not taken back.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.340
Bile acids follow the same rule, round a loop.

5
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.410
The liver makes them from cholesterol.

6
00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.890
Between meals they wait in the gallbladder.

7
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.590
After a meal they pour into the gut and form micelles, tiny carriers for fat.

8
00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.950
From the end of the small intestine, portal blood carries them back to the liver.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.800
Four names mark the stations.

10
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.060
CYP7A1 sets the pace of the classic pathway that makes them.

11
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.220
ASBT takes them back in the terminal ileum.

12
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.620
NTCP carries most uptake into liver cells.

13
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.680
BSEP is the main pump back into bile.

14
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:03.403
The pool is about 3.5 grams, sent round about 5 times a day.

15
00:01:04.543 --> 00:01:08.543
That delivers about 17.5 grams to the gut each day.

16
00:01:09.843 --> 00:01:13.243
Only 200 to 400 milligrams a day escape in stool.

17
00:01:14.383 --> 00:01:19.583
At steady state, the liver makes only what escapes, a few hundred milligrams a day.

18
00:01:20.863 --> 00:01:24.143
The rest of each day's delivery is recycled, not made.

19
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:26.667
Here is the one idea.

20
00:01:27.927 --> 00:01:31.267
A recycling loop needs new supply only for its residue.

21
00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:37.767
Capture, mostly in the ileum, sets that residue, and so sets how much the liver must

22
00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:38.767
make.

23
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.080
Now let capture slip to 90 percent a cycle.

24
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.000
Delivery stays 17.5 grams a day.

25
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.460
How much must the liver now make to keep up?

26
00:01:55.367 --> 00:02:00.607
Ten percent of 17.5 grams is 1.75 grams a day.

27
00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:04.127
That is about six times a mid-range loss.

28
00:02:05.407 --> 00:02:09.087
A modest slip in capture, once again, multiplies the residue.

29
00:02:10.217 --> 00:02:11.657
How does the liver know?

30
00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:17.417
Bile acids taken up in the ileum switch on a nuclear receptor, FXR.

31
00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:25.197
It induces a signal, FGF19, which reaches the liver and lowers CYP7A1.

32
00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:32.367
So when ileal recovery falls, less FGF19 arrives, the brake lifts, and synthesis

33
00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:36.047
rises. If it rises enough, the pool holds.

34
00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:40.767
If not, the pool settles smaller, where loss equals synthesis.

35
00:02:41.967 --> 00:02:43.667
So the liver is not the store.

36
00:02:44.887 --> 00:02:46.947
It only tops up what slips past the gut.

37
00:02:48.107 --> 00:02:50.927
The gut's own uptake sets the brake on that synthesis.
